When establishing infrastructure on the AWS cloud, Identity and Access Management (IAM) is among the first and most critical services to configure. 
IAM facilitates the creation and management of user accounts, groups, roles, policies, and other access controls. 
The Nautilus DevOps team is currently in the process of configuring these resources and has outlined the following requirements.

Create an IAM policy named iampolicy_yousuf in us-east-1 region using Terraform. 
It must allow read-only access to the EC2 console, i.e., this policy must allow users to view all instances, AMIs, and snapshots in the Amazon EC2 console.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform. Create the main.tf file (do not create a different .tf file) to accomplish this task.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
To create an IAM policy that grants read-only access to view EC2 instances, AMIs, and snapshots in the console, you will use the aws_iam_policy resource with a JSON policy document. The policy needs to allow the ec2:Describe* actions, which cover viewing these resources, as EC2 Describe actions do not support resource-level permissions and must use a wildcard resource .

Here is the main.tf for your task:

hcl
resource "aws_iam_policy" "iampolicy_yousuf" {
  name        = "iampolicy_yousuf"
  description = "Read-only access to view EC2 instances, AMIs, and snapshots in the console"

  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Action = [
          "ec2:Describe*"
        ]
        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}
💡 Key Details
Policy Name: Set exactly to iampolicy_yousuf as required.

Policy Document: The jsonencode function is used to format the policy correctly, as recommended by the Terraform documentation .

Actions: ec2:Describe* allows viewing all EC2 resources, including instances, AMIs, and snapshots. This is the standard way to provide read-only console access because Describe actions do not support granular resource-level permissions .

Region: IAM is a global service, so the us-east-1 region is handled by your provider configuration.

🚀 Execution Steps
Run the following in the terminal inside /home/bob/terraform:

bash
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Verify (optional)
bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 iam get-policy --policy-arn $(terraform state show aws_iam_policy.iampolicy_yousuf.arn | cut -d'"' -f2)
You should see the policy iampolicy_yousuf returned with the correct permissions.

